@@s001 Code: DFS in Java
@@s002 Code: DFS in Java
@@s003 The recursive DFS: visit, visit order, connected components and cycle detection. With the syntax notes on static fields and recursion.
@@s004 The recursive visit
@@s005 DFS is a function that calls itself on the unseen neighbours. The system's call stack does the work of the stack.
@@s006 The recursive visit
@@s007 The visit is a <code>static</code> method: it needs no object, it works on the static fields of the class.
@@s008 <code>++contatore</code> increments <b>before</b> reading: the first node visited gets 1.
@@s009 Unseen neighbour: I go down. The <code>!</code> negates the condition.
@@s010 I remember where I came from: it is needed so as not to confuse the parent with a cycle.
@@s011 Recursive call: at this point the node <code>u</code> waits on the stack.
@@s012 A neighbour already seen that is not the parent of <code>u</code>: it is a <b>back edge</b>, that is a cycle.
@@s013 Syntax notes
@@s014 <code>static</code>: the field or method belongs to the class, not to the object. Here <code>main</code> is static, so everything it uses is static.
@@s015 <code>!</code> is negation; a <code>boolean[]</code> is initialised entirely to <code>false</code>.
@@s016 In Java the depth of the recursion is limited: on graphs with millions of nodes an explicit stack is needed.
@@s017 Connected components
@@s018 The outer loop starts a DFS from each node not yet seen. Each start is a new component.
@@s019 Connected components
@@s020 Counter of the components.
@@s021 An unseen node is the start of a new component.
@@s022 I visit the whole component of <code>s</code>. When I return, all its nodes are marked.
@@s023 Syntax notes
@@s024 <code>componenti++</code> is <code>componenti = componenti + 1</code>.
